# Sides, Shaw, Grossmann & Lipsitz, *Campaigns and Elections* 5e — alignment notes

Working note, Aug 2026. Built from the Norton ereader: full chapter structure,
section headings, every captioned table, and each chapter's "Core of the
Analysis" claims. Supersedes the guesswork in `NOTES-debates.md`.

---

## 1. The book's own spine

The subtitle is the argument: **Rules, Reality, Strategy, Choice.** Chapter 1
sets those four up as a framework and every later chapter is an application of
it. Three consequences for us:

**(a) We already teach this, under different names.** The Tuesday four-move
structure (claim → evidence → disagreement → test) is a *method*; Rules /
Reality / Strategy / Choice is the book's *content taxonomy*. They compose
rather than compete. Worth naming the taxonomy explicitly in the syllabus so
students hear the same four words for thirteen weeks.

**(b) Chapter 1 hands us an evaluative rubric we are not using.** The book
judges campaigns against four democratic criteria — **free choice, political
equality, deliberation, freedom of speech** (Table 1.1 lays out what each
requires). Most chapters close with an "Evaluating…" section applying them.
This is a ready-made spine for the Tuesday debates, and it is *the book's own*,
which the debate reference sheet currently is not.

**(c) Every chapter ends with Key Terms and For Discussion.** Free test-prep and
free debate prompts. The For Discussion questions should be checked against my
debate mappings before Week 2.

## 2. Chapter structure

| Ch | Title | Notable structure |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Analyzing Campaigns and Elections | The four-part framework; the four democratic criteria |
| 2 | The American Electoral Process | Who can vote · How to vote · Who can run · When · **Where to run: single-member districts, reapportionment, redistricting** · Who wins |
| 3 | The Transformation of American Campaigns | **Five campaign eras** (1788–1824, 1828–92, 1896–1948, 1952–2012, 2016–present) + "The Case for Continuity" |
| 4 | Financing Campaigns | Rules for donors · rules for spenders · strategy · the reform debate |
| 5 | Modern Campaign Strategies | **Vote targets and profiling** · decision to run · messaging · whether to attack · where/how to campaign |
| 6 | Political Parties | Party-in-the-electorate / as-organization / in-government · "Are parties in decline?" · Party evolution and polarization |
| 7 | Interest Groups | Ballot initiatives · can groups deliver votes? · can they change the agenda? |
| 8 | Media | Nationalization · news as business · **which races get covered** · **news coverage of polls** · bias · **algorithms** |
| 9 | Presidential Campaigns | Nomination · conventions · **the Electoral College** · fall campaign |
| 10 | Congressional Campaigns | **Nationalization weakening the incumbency advantage** · candidate quality |
| 11 | State and Local Campaigns | Nonpartisan / at-large / **off-cycle elections** · ballot initiatives · judicial elections |
| 12 | Voter Participation | Ability · motivation · opportunity · **group differences: income, age, race, gender, race×gender** · are voters representative of nonvoters? |
| 13 | Voter Choice | Party ID · social groups · incumbent performance · issues · traits · **persuasion / reinforcement / priming** |

