This is the lab-relevant inventory. Most chapters have **no** captioned tables —
the book is much lighter on data display than its subtitle suggests, which is
itself the argument for this course existing.

| Table | Subject | Lab use |
|---|---|---|
| 1.1 | What the four democratic criteria require | Not data; a rubric. Use it in Week 2 discussion. |
| 4.1 | **Contribution limits, 2025–26 cycle** | **Week 5 FEC lab** — current-cycle limits, drop straight in |
| 4.2 | Groups with contribution limits | Week 5 |
| 4.3 | Independent groups | Week 5 |
| 5.1 | **NC gubernatorial turnout, 2012–2024** | **Week 6** — see §5 below |
| 5.2 | **NC gubernatorial votes won, 2012–2024** | **Week 6** |
| 5.3 | **Election results and vote targets, selected NC counties** | **Week 6 — the book's own worked targeting example** |
| 9.1 | **Electoral vote change from the 2020 Census** | **Week 3 apportionment lab** — but it sits in Ch 9 (see §4) |
| 9.2 | Harris/Trump broadcast ad spending, top 10 media markets | Week 6 |
| 9.3 | **Harris/Walz and Trump/Vance appearances, top 5 states** | **Week 6 campaign-visits lab** — but again Ch 9 |
| 12.1 | **Effectiveness and cost-per-voter of campaign activities** | **Week 10 — strong new lab candidate** |

## 4. Problems with the current schedule

**(a) The apportionment and visits labs depend on Chapter 9, which we read in
Week 11.** Table 9.1 is the 2020 Census electoral-vote shift — that *is* the
Week 3 lab. Table 9.3 is candidate appearances by state — that *is* the Week 6
lab. Both are eight and five weeks ahead of the reading. Options:

  1. Move the Electoral College portion of Ch 9 up to Week 3 alongside Ch 2
     (Ch 2 already covers reapportionment, so it is a natural pairing).
  2. Leave it; tell students in Week 3 and 6 that they are seeing Ch 9's data
     early and will meet the argument in November.
  3. Move Ch 9 to Week 6 and give Week 11 only Ch 10–11.

  **DONE — went with (1).** Ch 2's "Where to Run" already covers reapportionment and
  redistricting; adding the Electoral College section of Ch 9 makes Week 3 a
  complete unit on how votes convert to seats, which is what the lab does.

**(b) Chapter 5 is better lab material than the current Week 6 lab uses.**
Tables 5.1–5.3 are a worked vote-targeting example on North Carolina
gubernatorial races 2012–2024 — turnout, votes, and county-level targets. Our
Week 6 lab is campaign *visits*. Both are Ch 5-adjacent, but the NC tables let
students reproduce the book's own arithmetic and then break it. See §5.

**(c) Ch 10's headline claim contradicts a thing students believe.**
"Congressional elections have been nationalized, weakening the incumbency
advantage" — that is a testable claim, and Week 11's election-night lab already
computes a baseline for 433 districts. Direct hit; make it the Week 11 debate
rather than the generic centralization one.

**(d) Ch 12 is far richer than "turnout."** It breaks participation down by
income, age, race, gender, **and race × gender**, and asks whether voters
represent nonvoters. That is a better fit for our BISG/surnames material
(Week 14) than its current Week 10 slot suggests. At minimum the Week 10
discussion should preview Week 14.

**(e) Ch 8 covers "news coverage of polls" and algorithms.** Both connect Week 9
to Week 10 more tightly than the current syllabus says.

## 5. Lab plan changes

All fourteen labs are built and knit. These are refinements, not rebuilds.

| Lab | Change |
|---|---|
| **Wk 3 apportionment** | Add Table 9.1 as the book's version of the same result. Students compare our Huntington–Hill output to the book's table — a free accuracy check, and it surfaces that the book gives the answer without the method. |
| **Wk 5 FEC** | Replace hand-entered limits with **Table 4.1** (2025–26 cycle). Currently the lab uses 2024 figures. |
| **Wk 6 visits** | Add **Table 9.3** (appearances, top 5 states) as a cross-check against our AP-tracker count of 324 stops. If they disagree — and they will, because the book counts differently — that discrepancy *is* the lesson. |
| **BUILT — `vote-targeting/`** (moved to Wk 4) | Built from **Tables 5.1–5.3**. Reproduce the book's NC county vote targets, then re-run with 2024 turnout and ask what the targets should have been. This is the single best lab-from-the-book opportunity in the whole text. |
| **BUILT — `gotv/`** | **Table 12.1** gives cost-per-voter by campaign activity. Combine with a budget constraint: spend $250k to maximise votes. Tiny lab, big payoff, pure interpretation. |
| **Wk 11 election night** | Reframe Option B around Ch 10's nationalization claim rather than generic centralization. |

## 6. Supplemental readings and podcasts — gaps

The five podcast episodes now in the schedule (Weeks 9, 10, 11, 13, 15) all hold
up against the chapters. Remaining gaps, now that I know what each chapter
actually argues:

- **Ch 3, five campaign eras** — wants one historical piece. The book's era
  boundaries (1828, 1896, 1952, 2016) are contestable and a good argument.
- **Ch 7 interest groups** — nothing assigned. Ballot-initiative spending is the
  most teachable angle and connects to Ch 11.
- **Ch 11 state and local** — **off-cycle elections** is the most under-covered
  high-impact topic in the book and we give it no reading. This is where the
  turnout inequality argument bites hardest.
- **Ch 8 algorithms** — our media lab is Wikipedia pageviews; the chapter is
  about algorithmic curation. Adjacent but not the same.

## 7. Debate mappings — recheck against the book

`NOTES-debates.md` was written before I could read the text and says so. Now
checkable. Two changes I am confident about already:

- **Week 2 (Ch 1)** should use the book's own four democratic criteria, not an
  outside debate. Ch 1 *is* a framework chapter and Table 1.1 is the prompt.
- **Week 11 (Ch 10)** should be nationalization vs. local, which is Ch 10's
  own headline claim and testable in that day's lab.

The rest need the For Discussion questions read against the reference sheet.


